      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. The dose looks unsafe, and I'd like it confirmed first.\n\n- **The dose is too high.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. The usual maximum for adults is 2,550 mg/day (2,000 mg/day for extended-release in some labeling). At 5,100 mg/day, the risk of serious harm, including lactic acidosis, goes up substantially, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n- **It may be a typo.** Common regimens are 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg/day) or 500 mg or 1,000 mg twice daily. If 1700 mg was meant as a total daily dose, it could be given as 850 mg twice daily.\n\nOrders like this normally come from the treating prescriber. If you're relaying it for one, please have them confirm the intended dose and frequency. Once I have a corrected or confirmed order, I can enter it right away. For example, I could enter metformin 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals."
      }
     ],
